{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed168776016/2/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"984d8a3e\" lines=\"5-21\">\n<description>\nShift each row's dominant middle-color run left until its right edge aligns with the leftmost original right edge, moving displaced left-side pixels after the run.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has three colors.\n- The most common color forms one contiguous horizontal run in every row.\n- Pixels before those runs share one side color, and pixels after those runs share the other side color.\n\nRule:\n- Find the minimum right edge column among the middle-color runs.\n- In each row, keep the run width and the trailing right-side color count.\n- Place the middle-color run so its right edge is at that minimum column, unless the run is too wide, in which case start it at column 0.\n- Fill any remaining non-trailing cells with the left-side color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe varying row prefixes are movable padding; the stable feature is the leftmost right boundary of the middle-color shape.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"16b78196\" lines=\"6-20\">\n<description>\nAssemble colored sprites into a central bar's notches, chaining successive pieces along fixed-width strips.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Largest monochrome component is a solid \"bar\" with boundary notches (bg holes opening to its bbox edge).\n- Several smaller colored sprites scattered around the bar.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar notch is filled by a root piece whose shape exactly covers the hole.\n- The root defines a strip (column-span or row-span) for its chain.\n- Successive pieces fill the bg cells inside the previous piece's bbox, locked to the same strip width.\n- Multiple chains grow independently from different bar notches.\n\nKey insight: The root piece's extent perpendicular to the chain direction locks the strip width for all subsequent pieces in that chain.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9b5080bb\" lines=\"161-172\">\n<description>\nRecolor the small defect bars on each shared-color object with the opposite panel color.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid contains two large panel colors and one shared object color.\n- Each shared-color object is a mostly solid rectangle inside exactly one panel.\nRule\n- Find the near-solid rectangular core of each object.\n- Missing core pixels and one-cell protrusions define two-pixel bars perpendicular to the nearest core edge.\n- Recolor those bars with the other panel color; leave all other pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\n- The colors to add are not tied to numeric ids: each object's surrounding panel identifies the opposite panel color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b1986d4b\" lines=\"19-37\">\n<description>\nRender the filled-square object counts as compact nested stacks ordered by count rank.\n\nInput structure\nA large noisy grid has a dominant background, scattered non-square noise, and\nfilled monochrome square objects. Each square side length has exactly one\nobject color, and side lengths form consecutive sizes.\n\nRule\nIgnore non-square components. If the lowest square in the scene is of the\nsmallest side length, treat that one smallest square as a distractor and do\nnot count it. For count rank k from left to right, create one block from all\nsquare sizes with at least k remaining objects; draw larger squares first and\nsmaller squares over their upper-left corner, then append one background\nseparator column.\n\nKey insight\nThe output is a count histogram of square side lengths, but each histogram\nslot is rendered as nested upper-left square masks using the side colors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9bbf930d\" lines=\"61-72\">\n<description>\nMove magenta particles along corridors formed by parallel colored bars.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg=7. Particles=6 in col 0. Colored bars on even rows form corridors on odd rows.\n\nRule:\n- Particle goes straight through bg cells.\n- When blocked by a colored cell, turns 90° into the perpendicular direction\n  that has a bg cell flanked by same-color bars. Stops at grid edge or full blockage.\n\nKey insight: Same-color parallel bars define the only valid corridors, so each particle follows a deterministic local trace.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"42f83767\" lines=\"23-39\">\n<description>\nExpand the lower color grid by replacing each pixel with its matching top stencil.\n\nInput structure\nThe input has a top legend and a lower dense color grid, separated by blank rows.\nThe legend contains left-to-right 2x2 color swatches and, to their right,\nthe same number of square neutral-color stencil masks.\n\nRule\nPair the swatches and stencil masks by left-to-right order. For every pixel in\nthe lower color grid, paste the paired stencil mask as one output block,\nrecoloring the mask pixels with that pixel's color and leaving mask holes as bg.\n\nKey insight\nThe lower grid is not copied directly; it is an index grid whose colors select\nthe stencil shape from the legend, so each input pixel becomes one square block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"98c475bf\" lines=\"62-76\">\n<description>\nErase the complete interior glyph and expand each endpoint marker pair into its color's full glyph.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is 20x20 with background inside two same-color full-height vertical frame bars.\n- Inside the frame there is one complete color glyph and one or more other colors appearing only as two endpoint markers in the same row.\n- Marker endpoints are always in the first and last interior columns.\n\nRule\n- Keep the vertical frame unchanged.\n- Erase any complete interior glyph.\n- For each endpoint marker pair, use that marker color's learned glyph template and anchor its horizontal bar on the marker row.\n\nKey insight\n- Color identifies which glyph shape to draw; the two endpoint pixels are not copied objects, they are anchors for reconstructing that color's full symbol.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1b59e163\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nCopy each marked sprite to every standalone marker of the same color and erase the original sources.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each source is an 8-connected component containing a monochrome sprite and one marker pixel of another color.\n- Other isolated marker pixels may be destinations; marker colors with no source sprite are distractors.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the grid to background.\n- For every source marker color, place the sprite at each other marker of that color using the source marker as the anchor.\n- Keep each destination marker pixel in place; do not keep source sprites, source markers, or distractor markers.\n\nKey insight:\n- A colored marker is not part of the sprite geometry; it is the anchor that says where same-colored copies of that sprite should be stamped.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"13f06aa5\" lines=\"36-51\">\n<description>\nExtend each colored marker from its directional sprite to a grid edge, painting a dotted ray and the whole edge side.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One repeated body color forms small 8-connected directional sprites.\n- Each marker is a singleton non-background, non-body color embedded in one sprite.\n\nRule:\n- The body pixels around a marker lie mostly behind it; the opposite direction is the marker's ray direction.\n- Paint every second pixel from the marker along that ray, then paint the entire boundary side reached by the ray.\n- If two painted boundary sides meet at a corner, make that shared corner black.\n\nKey insight:\n- The same seven-pixel body sprite rotates to encode the direction; its local center-of-mass bias points away from the ray.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1478ab18\" lines=\"35-43\">\n<description>\nPaint the teal outline of the right triangle determined by the missing corner of the grey-marker square.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is an orange background with exactly four foreground marker pixels of one color. The markers have a square bounding box: three markers are bbox corners, and the fourth marker is inside that square.\nRule\nFind the one corner of the marker bounding square that has no marker. That missing corner is the right-angle vertex of a right isosceles triangle; paint its two axis-aligned legs and the opposite diagonal in teal, leaving existing marker pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\nThe present corner marker opposite the missing corner is a decoy; the three triangle vertices are the missing corner and the two marker corners adjacent to it.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e734a0e8\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nCopy the unique colored sprite tile into every tiled cell marked by an internal separator-colored pixel.\n\nInput structure\n- Full same-color separator rows and columns partition the grid into equal rectangular tiles.\n- One tile contains the only non-background, non-separator colored sprite.\n- Target tiles are otherwise background-colored but contain at least one internal pixel of the separator color.\n\nRule\n- Keep the separator lines and the source sprite tile unchanged.\n- Replace each marked target tile with an exact copy of the source tile.\n- Unmarked background-only tiles remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The internal separator-colored pixels are placement markers; the single colored tile is the template to stamp into those marked cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"84551f4c\" lines=\"42-53\">\n<description>\nRotate each blue-started chain of bottom-aligned vertical bars into horizontal bars resting on the same baseline.\nInput structure\n- The grid has a background and bottom-aligned, one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Blue bars are markers; red bars are body bars that may be chained after a marker.\nRule\n- Starting at each blue bar, repeatedly include the next red bar whose column is exactly the current bar's column plus the current bar's height.\n- Clear every included vertical bar, then redraw each included bar horizontally to the right from its original bottom cell, with length equal to its vertical height.\n- Red bars that are not reached from a blue marker remain unchanged.\nKey insight\n- A vertical bar's height is also the width it occupies after rotation, so the chain follows immediate horizontal contact after each rotated bar.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"54dc2872\" lines=\"52-69\">\n<description>\nMove each marked L-shaped sprite onto the separate pixel matching its marker color.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground has 4-connected multicolor sprites and isolated one-pixel controls.\n- Each sprite has one repeated body color in an L shape plus one embedded marker\n  pixel of a different color.\n\nRule\n- If the embedded marker color also appears as a separate isolated control pixel,\n  translate the whole sprite so the body L-bend lands on that control pixel.\n- Clear each moved sprite from its old position, clear its consumed control pixel,\n  then paste all translated sprites; sprites without a matching control stay put.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker color pairs a sprite with its destination control, while the L-bend\n  gives the orientation-independent anchor cell to place on that destination.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b25e450b\" lines=\"28-43\">\n<description>\nSlide each black border rectangle to the opposite border, recoloring its swept stripe orange and leaving the rectangle black at the far edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid contains black rectangular 4-connected masks, each touching exactly one outer border.\n- Grey cells form incidental pattern content; orange is the trail color used when a black mask slides.\n\nRule\n- Treat all black rectangles as moving simultaneously.\n- A rectangle touching the top or bottom edge moves vertically to the opposite edge in the same columns.\n- A rectangle touching the left or right edge moves horizontally to the opposite edge in the same rows.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
